  "verdict": "Cadence is not the explanation for the gap. On identical physical times, identical sample counts and an identical estimator, dt=1.0 gives +4.2 and dt=0.5 gives +0.4 (t>=2000), and the ordering holds in every matched window; the two dt=1.0 baselines agree on their common window. What remains is a difference between two discrete systems, not between two samplings of one. But the decimation table shows something else that we have to say out loud: the magnitude at dt=1.0 is itself placement-sensitive (+3.69 with 13 samples, +2.79 with 7, +1.00 with 4), because the contrast decays with time and a coarse quadrature weights the late low-contrast part more heavily. So the robust part of the result is the gap, not the number +3.57, which is a property of the estimator at this cadence.",
  "not_claimed": [
    "this does not identify the mechanism (modified equation vs. genuine discrete dynamics); it removes one candidate",
    "the decimation table changes sample count and quadrature placement together; it does not separate them",
    "the seed bootstrap measures seed variability only, not systematic uncertainty of the field",
    "grid 96 is below the range Pearson checked (256-1024); nothing here speaks about the continuum",
    "counts at one fixed threshold 0.2 remain threshold-dependent (see l1_field_percolation)"
  ]
}
